.Dd August 16, 2026
.Dt BSDTRACE 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm bsdtrace
.Nd hardware-assisted execution tracing using Intel Processor Trace
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm exec
.Op Ar options
.Fl -
.Ar command Op Ar args
.Nm
.Cm trace
.Op Ar options
.Ar pid
.Nm
.Cm list
.Op Fl p Ar pid
.Nm
.Cm decode
.Op Fl f Ar format
.Ar trace.pt
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
uses Intel Processor Trace (PT) via the
.Xr hwt 4
framework to capture every branch a program takes, then decodes the
trace into symbolized control-flow events: function calls, returns,
jumps, and system calls.
Traces are saved to disk for offline analysis — capture once,
re-decode as many times as needed.
.Ss Subcommands
.Bl -tag -width "decode"
.It Cm exec
Run a command under tracing.
.It Cm trace
Attach to a running process.
.It Cm list
Show HWT availability and backend capabilities; with
.Fl p ,
list a process's threads.
.It Cm decode
Offline re-decode a saved
.Pa .pt
+
.Pa .meta
file pair.
.El
.Ss Options
.Bl -tag -width "-r range"
.It Fl f Ar format
Output format:
.Cm text
(default),
.Cm json ,
.Cm profile ,
.Cm tree ,
or
.Cm collapsed
(folded stacks for flame graphs).
.It Fl d Ar seconds
Trace duration
.Po Fl t
also accepted;
.Cm exec
default: 30
.Pc .
.It Fl s Ar size
PT buffer size, e.g.\&
.Li 8m ,
.Li 64m
(default: 64m).
.It Fl o Ar file
Output path for the
.Pa .pt
file.
.It Fl r Ar range
IP filter:
.Li 0xstart:0xend
(end exclusive) or a function name (up to 2 ranges); prefix with
.Ql stop\&:
for TraceStop mode.
.It Fl T Ar tid
Thread index (default: 0), comma list
.Pq Li 0,1,3 ,
or
.Cm all .
.It Fl P Ar freq
PSB sync frequency 0-15.
.It Fl C
Enable timing packets (MTC + cycle-accurate).
.It Fl M Ar freq
Explicit MTC frequency 1-15.
.It Fl Y Ar thresh
Explicit CYC threshold 1-15.
.It Fl W
Enable PTWRITE trace markers (see
.In bsdtrace_ptwrite.h ) .
.It Fl K
Include OS/kernel-mode tracing.
.It Fl m Ar count
Stop after N HWT records.
.It Fl b Ar backend
HWT backend (default: auto-detect).
.It Fl A
Disable ASLR for the child process
.Pq Cm exec
only.
.It Fl n
Dry run — validate setup without tracing.
.It Fl p
Pause the target on mmap/exec events.
.El
.Sh OUTPUT
Each trace produces
.Pa bsdtrace-<pid>.pt
(raw Intel PT data) and
.Pa bsdtrace-<pid>.meta
(binary mapping metadata as JSONL); with
.Fl T Cm all ,
additional per-thread
.Pa -tid<N>
file pairs are written.
.Sh REQUIREMENTS
A kernel with
.Cd options HWT_HOOKS
and the
.Xr hwt 4
and pt kernel modules loaded, on a CPU with Intel Processor Trace.
Intel PT is an Intel-only CPU feature; AMD processors are not
supported.
The
.Xr hwt 4
framework itself is machine-independent and also supports ARM SPE on
arm64, but
.Nm
requires the Intel PT backend.
Tracing requires root.
Offline
.Cm decode
needs no special privileges or hardware.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Trace a command for 5 seconds:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bsdtrace exec -t 5 -- /bin/sleep 1
.Ed
.Pp
Attach to a running process, all threads, with timing:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bsdtrace trace -T all -C -d 10 $(pgrep -n nginx)
.Ed
.Pp
Re-decode a saved trace as folded stacks for a flame graph:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bsdtrace decode -f collapsed bsdtrace-1234.pt | \e
    flamegraph.pl > trace.svg
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr bsdinstruments 8 ,
.Xr hwtlm 8
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
first appeared in the ObservableBSD project.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Kory Heard
